While experimenting with this shift register while at the max clock frequency, I actually ran into several timing violations and errors. For one, the design obviously requires the 
  clock to be able to activate it's ANDs with a given input as well. While that's fine, I actually did not realize that what I made earlier was NOT a D flip-flop* but more of a clock-sensitive 
  D-latch. The next iteration seeks to resolve this but for now, this told us a lot, design-wise. The next thing I discovered was the propagation delay between the latches. As we know, increasing 
  the amount of logic a circuit needs to run also increases the time it takes for the logic to pass through it. With that being said, 0.1s/0.1s was actually found to be too fast for two reasons.

The first reason is that the circuit, while most times works, might glitch or pass a new state before the last latch was done processing a bit. Although its rudimentary logic, everything at the small
  scale is amplified at larger scales. The second reason this clock speed was too fast is because of how Oxygen Not Included works. ONI is a game, and in the game, it SIMULATES logic. What I mean by that
  is that we can create logic to stunning accuracy, but we start to lose entire rises and falls of the clock if the peroid is too low. Through testing, I found that 0.1s/0.3 seems to work fine as 0.1s-0.3s
  seems to be easily enough to pass a signal and capture it (remember that we accidently created a D Latch as opposed to D Flip-flops) while +0.3s gave enough time for the propegation delay to process
  the bits as well as sned it to the next latch. This otherwise caps our clock speed to 0.4s periods or 2.5 Hz.

The second idea to generate an address is actually much simplier and clearer, but utilizaes a 4-bit ribbon as opposed to the standard automation wire. The process was relatively the same--load a static
  address into something that would hold it and then send it forward. This time, instead of creating a shift register, I used 4 ANDs and 4 switches that correlate to the planetoid's address (think 0001.)
  We also utilized a counter to measure the state we were in via clock pulses, although that needs more tweaking and experimenting. The biggest difference was that instead of pushing a serial code through
  flip-flops (think, push 0, push 1, push 0, and so on), we used ribbon writers as the outputs to a 4-bit ribbon respectively. This actually cut down on a lot of space and wiring/complexity overhead so 
